print(x + 3)
print(y + "3")', 'python', '', '`x` holds the number 7 (an `int`), and `y` holds the text `"7"` (a `str`), because of the quotes.

- `x + 3` adds two numbers: **10**.
- `y + "3"` joins two Strings: `"7"` followed by `"3"` is **73**. It is text, not the number seventy-three.

Mixing them, as in `y + 3`, would stop with a `TypeError`: Python won''t join text to a number.', 'medium', 'auto', '{"compare":"trim_trailing","caseSensitive":true}', 1, 'published', 0, 1792108800000) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET topic_id = excluded.topic_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, slug = excluded.slug, type = excluded.type, title = excluded.title, prompt_md = excluded.prompt_md, code = excluded.code, code_language = excluded.code_language, expected_behavior_md = excluded.expected_behavior_md, explanation_md = excluded.explanation_md, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, grading_mode = excluded.grading_mode, settings = excluded.settings, points = excluded.points, status = excluded.status, is_demo = excluded.is_demo, updated_at = excluded.updated_at;
