INSERT INTO exercises (id, course_id, topic_id, lesson_id, slug, type, title, prompt_md, code, code_language, expected_behavior_md, explanation_md, difficulty, grading_mode, settings, points, status, is_demo, updated_at) VALUES ('exe_adc9a5ba18215adf', 'course_programming_fundamentals', 'top_e519989412df8f8b', 'les_0a16fb429d30296a', 'final-m3-exam-fix-one-mark-five-times', 'correct_code', 'One mark judged five times', 'A teacher types the marks of **5** students. For each student the program should print `PASS` (50 or more) or `FAIL`, and at the end print how many passed and how many failed.

For the marks `72 45 50 38 90` it should print PASS, FAIL, PASS, FAIL, PASS, then `Passed: 3` and `Failed: 2`. Instead it prints `PASS` five times, then `Passed: 5` and `Failed: 1`.

The program has **two** bugs. Find them and fix the program. Keep the `while` loop and its counter, and don''t add new variables.', '#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
    int counter = 1;
    int passCount = 0;
    int mark;
    cin >> mark;
    while (counter <= 5)
    {
        if (mark >= 50)
        {
            cout << "PASS" << endl;
            passCount = passCount + 1;
        }
        else
        {
            cout << "FAIL" << endl;
        }
        counter = counter + 1;
    }
    cout << "Passed: " << passCount << endl;
    cout << "Failed: " << counter - passCount << endl;
    return 0;
}', 'cpp', 'The user types 5 whole-number marks. For each mark, in order, the program prints `PASS` if it is 50 or more, otherwise `FAIL`. Then it prints `Passed: ` and the number of passes, and `Failed: ` and the number of fails.', '1. **The mark is read only once.** `cin >> mark;` is **before** the loop, so it runs once and reads 72. The loop then judges the same 72 five times: PASS, PASS, PASS, PASS, PASS. The other four marks are never read. The read must be the first line **inside** the loop body, so every pass reads a new mark and then makes the decision about it: read, process, repeat.
2. **The counter is one too big after the loop.** The loop stops when `counter <= 5` becomes FALSE, which happens when `counter` is **6**. So `counter - passCount` gives one fail too many. There were 5 students, so the number of fails is `5 - passCount` (or `counter - 1 - passCount`).

Fixing only the first bug is not enough: the lecture''s marks would then print `Passed: 3` but `Failed: 3`, because 6 − 3 = 3, and 3 + 3 is more than the 5 students.', 'hard', 'reference_review', '{"reserved":true,"compare":"trim_trailing"}', 1, 'published', 0, 1791590400000) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET topic_id = excluded.topic_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, slug = excluded.slug, type = excluded.type, title = excluded.title, prompt_md = excluded.prompt_md, code = excluded.code, code_language = excluded.code_language, expected_behavior_md = excluded.expected_behavior_md, explanation_md = excluded.explanation_md, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, grading_mode = excluded.grading_mode, settings = excluded.settings, points = excluded.points, status = excluded.status, is_demo = excluded.is_demo, updated_at = excluded.updated_at;
