INSERT INTO exercises (id, course_id, topic_id, lesson_id, slug, type, title, prompt_md, code, code_language, expected_behavior_md, explanation_md, difficulty, grading_mode, settings, points, status, is_demo, updated_at) VALUES ('exe_62aceb7dcd53d636', 'course_programming_fundamentals', 'top_e519989412df8f8b', 'les_0a16fb429d30296a', 'final-m4-exam-which-marks-were-typed', 'mcq', 'Which marks were typed?', 'This is the exam results processor with the lecture''s **bonus**: for each of the 5 marks it prints `EVEN` or `ODD`, then `PASS` or `FAIL`, and at the end the number of passes.

```pseudocode
BEGIN
    counter ← 1
    passCount ← 0
    WHILE counter <= 5 DO
        INPUT mark
        IF mark % 2 == 0 THEN
            PRINT "EVEN"
        ELSE
            PRINT "ODD"
        END IF
        IF mark >= 50 THEN
            PRINT "PASS"
            passCount ← passCount + 1
        ELSE
            PRINT "FAIL"
        END IF
        counter ← counter + 1
    END WHILE
    PRINT passCount
END
```

A teacher ran it, and apart from the marks she typed, it printed these 11 lines:

```output
EVEN
FAIL
ODD
PASS
ODD
FAIL
EVEN
PASS
EVEN
PASS
3
```

Which lists of marks could she have typed? Pick **all** of them.', NULL, NULL, '', 'Work backwards. In every pass the loop prints exactly two lines, so the first 10 lines are 5 pairs, one per mark:

| Mark | Lines | So the mark is… |
|---|---|---|
| 1st | `EVEN`, `FAIL` | even, and less than 50 |
| 2nd | `ODD`, `PASS` | odd, and 50 or more |
| 3rd | `ODD`, `FAIL` | odd, and less than 50 |
| 4th | `EVEN`, `PASS` | even, and 50 or more |
| 5th | `EVEN`, `PASS` | even, and 50 or more |

The last line, `3`, is printed once, after the loop, and agrees with the three `PASS` pairs.

Now test each list against the table. `48 51 49 50 100` and `0 77 1 62 90` fit every row (0 is even, because nothing is left over when 0 is divided by 2). `50 51 …` breaks the first row, because 50 is a pass, not a fail: `>=` includes the boundary. `… 99` breaks the last row, because 99 is odd.

Reading an output backwards like this is a good test of whether you really understand what each line of the algorithm does.', 'hard', 'auto', '{"reserved":true,"multiple":true,"shuffle":true}', 1, 'published', 0, 1791590400000) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET topic_id = excluded.topic_id, lesson_id = excluded.lesson_id, slug = excluded.slug, type = excluded.type, title = excluded.title, prompt_md = excluded.prompt_md, code = excluded.code, code_language = excluded.code_language, expected_behavior_md = excluded.expected_behavior_md, explanation_md = excluded.explanation_md, difficulty = excluded.difficulty, grading_mode = excluded.grading_mode, settings = excluded.settings, points = excluded.points, status = excluded.status, is_demo = excluded.is_demo, updated_at = excluded.updated_at;
